{"archetypes":[{"name":"shell-kv-protocol","focus_area":"correctness","weight":2,"rationale":"The diff introduces two KV-parsing handoff loops (file-first + stdout merge) in SKILL.md for both drivers; values containing '=' could be mis-split, and WARN/ERROR accumulation vs immediate-print semantics must be symmetric between file and stdout paths.","prompt_body":"Audit every `while IFS= read -r _line` KV-parsing loop in `skills/design/SKILL.md` Step 0b for the route and init fences. Check whether `_value=\"${_line#*=}\"` correctly handles values that themselves contain `=` (e.g. `TITLE_FILTER_MARKER=[IMPLEMENTING] foo`). Verify that WARN and ERROR are printed as breadcrumbs immediately in the file-first loop but NOT stored via `printf -v`, and that the second stdout-merge loop also prints WARN/ERROR as breadcrumbs and does NOT fill them into variables — confirm the two loops are consistent. Check whether `_route_warn_lines` / `_route_error_lines` arrays accumulate duplicates when both file and stdout carry the same WARN line (pause-load fallthrough path). Verify `<<<"${_route_out:-}"` and `<<<"${_init_out:-}"` correctly produce an empty here-string (no spurious blank line) when the driver emits nothing. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"bash-arithmetic-guard","focus_area":"correctness","weight":2,"rationale":"SKILL.md computes `MARKER_REMAINING=$((MARKER_TTL - MARKER_AGE))` on values read from the result-env, which are initialised to empty strings and may stay empty if the driver exits before writing them; empty-string arithmetic is a fatal bash error.","prompt_body":"Locate every bash arithmetic expansion `$((...))` in `skills/design/SKILL.md` Step 0b (especially `MARKER_REMAINING=$((MARKER_TTL - MARKER_AGE))` in the `cancel-reentry-guard` branch) and in `scripts/test-design-structure.sh` (e.g. `if (( init_refresh_line >= init_rename_line || init_refresh_line >= init_run_params_line ))`). For each, trace whether the operand variables are guaranteed non-empty and numeric at the point of use: in SKILL.md, `MARKER_TTL` and `MARKER_AGE` are initialised to `\"\"` and only set by KV parsing — check whether they can remain empty when the `cancel-reentry-guard` branch is taken. In `test-design-structure.sh`, check whether `grep -nF` piped through `head -1 | cut -d: -f1` can return an empty string when the pattern is absent, and whether the surrounding `[[ -n … ]]` guards fire before the `(( … ))` test. Also check `plan_block_present` in `skills/design/scripts/design-route.sh` around lines 1531-1533 where `start_line` and `end_line` are assigned from `grep -n` output and then compared with `-ge` — confirm that the earlier `[[ \"$start_count\" -eq 1 && \"$end_count\" -eq 1 ]]` guards exclude the empty-string case. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"driver-exit-contract","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"The route and init drivers have asymmetric exit-code semantics (exit 1 means op-failure for route but contract-drift for init), and the orchestrator's handling of _init_rc=1 with empty INIT_STATUS creates a silent non-exit path that may be exploitable.","prompt_body":"Verify the exit-code contract between `skills/design/scripts/design-route.sh`, `skills/design/scripts/design-init-runparams.sh`, and the SKILL.md Step 0b fences. For the init fence: confirm what happens when `_init_rc=1` but `INIT_STATUS` is empty (never populated by the KV loops, e.g. because the result env write failed before emitting `INIT_STATUS`); the check `[[ \"${_init_rc:-0}\" -eq 1 && \"$INIT_STATUS\" == contract-drift ]]` would not fire, and the code would fall through to continue — verify whether this is a silent success on a failed init. For the route fence: confirm `_route_rc` path that is non-zero and not 2 aborts before any KV read; verify the `[[ \"${_route_rc:-0}\" -ne 0 ]]` guard fires before the file-first loop. Also check whether `phase_driver_write_result_env` atomicity (writing to a tmp then mv) prevents a partial result env from being read when the driver is re-run on replay — look at how `RESULT_ENV` is overwritten if it already exists from a previous run. Check the `emit_route_result` function exits with `exit 0` after calling `phase_driver_write_result_env`, confirming no additional output leaks to the caller after the result env is written. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
